WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:05.500
there is going to be a staggering amount of
disinformation spread around the 2020 election.

00:00:05.500 --> 00:00:08.910
This includes the primary, this includes the
general election.

00:00:08.910 --> 00:00:11.800
Once we have a democratic presidential nominee.

00:00:11.800 --> 00:00:12.800
Now

00:00:12.800 --> 00:00:17.780
I do my best to point out specific instances
of disinformation and try to debunk them on

00:00:17.780 --> 00:00:18.780
the show.

00:00:18.780 --> 00:00:23.970
So for example, yesterday on the show, we
looked at this, uh, a sort of meme spread

00:00:23.970 --> 00:00:30.180
by James Woods and Charlie Kirk and others
about Bernie Sanders and his tax plan and

00:00:30.180 --> 00:00:31.470
his healthcare plan.

00:00:31.470 --> 00:00:36.041
But we can't rely on debunkings in media because
we just simply can't keep up.

00:00:36.041 --> 00:00:42.280
I mean, it takes five seconds to tweet out,
disinformation, and then it took me 10 minutes

00:00:42.280 --> 00:00:46.200
to debunk it for you and explain to you all
the ways in which it was wrong and the way

00:00:46.200 --> 00:00:48.710
Bernie's tax plan will actually work.

00:00:48.710 --> 00:00:54.629
So we all need strategies to protect ourselves
from the disinformation instead of hoping

00:00:54.629 --> 00:00:57.340
that you come across someone else.

00:00:57.340 --> 00:00:58.520
Debunking disinformation.

00:00:58.520 --> 00:01:02.809
There's a new report from Stanford which says
that we're going to see a lot of the same

00:01:02.809 --> 00:01:07.450
techniques from 2016 used spread disinformation
in 2020 plus.

00:01:07.450 --> 00:01:12.270
There will be new, more sophisticated techniques
that have surfaced over the last four years,

00:01:12.270 --> 00:01:16.360
and they prey on this role.

00:01:16.360 --> 00:01:18.009
Emotional reactions.

00:01:18.009 --> 00:01:23.999
That is one of the main ways that this information
spreads, which is you see something and your,

00:01:23.999 --> 00:01:29.590
your emotional reaction makes you immediately
share it before you've actually vetted what

00:01:29.590 --> 00:01:31.049
it is that you are sharing.

00:01:31.049 --> 00:01:33.939
So the acronym to remember is caps.

00:01:33.939 --> 00:01:38.200
You know like Trump uses cap slok during a
lot of his tweets.

00:01:38.200 --> 00:01:39.200
Caps is the acronym.

00:01:39.200 --> 00:01:43.639
I used this when I was teaching at Boston
college in the media literacy section of the

00:01:43.639 --> 00:01:44.639
class.

00:01:44.639 --> 00:01:49.039
And what this means is critically analyze
primary sources.

00:01:49.039 --> 00:01:52.679
Caps, critically analyze primary sources.

00:01:52.679 --> 00:01:56.700
It's extremely simple, but it is pretty all
encompassing.

00:01:56.700 --> 00:02:01.059
If you apply it correctly, you only need to
ask two questions whenever you're presented

00:02:01.059 --> 00:02:03.399
with any claim in media.

00:02:03.399 --> 00:02:07.689
Am I looking at a primary source and have
I critically analyzed it?

00:02:07.689 --> 00:02:13.620
So as an example, if we look at that viral
disinformation tweet from yesterday that was

00:02:13.620 --> 00:02:17.680
spread by James Woods and Charlie Kirk and
other right-wingers.

00:02:17.680 --> 00:02:23.879
So first of all, we look through it and look
at is anything here a primary source?

00:02:23.879 --> 00:02:28.790
So when the thing says, Bernie said at the
debate, he wants a $15 minimum wage.

00:02:28.790 --> 00:02:35.400
Is it a primary source when it's an unsighted,
uh, Twitter image sent out by James Woods?

00:02:35.400 --> 00:02:36.400
No.

00:02:36.400 --> 00:02:37.400
So go to Bernie's website and find it.

00:02:37.400 --> 00:02:39.379
Now it happens that, that, that is accurate.

00:02:39.379 --> 00:02:42.080
Bernie does want a $15 minimum wage.

00:02:42.080 --> 00:02:43.209
Then you look at the numbers.

00:02:43.209 --> 00:02:47.969
Well, $15 an hour times 40 hours a week times
52 weeks a year.

00:02:47.969 --> 00:02:49.079
Critically analyze it.

00:02:49.079 --> 00:02:51.660
Does everyone work 40 hours in their job?

00:02:51.660 --> 00:02:52.660
No.

00:02:52.660 --> 00:02:54.390
Does everyone work 52 weeks in a year?

00:02:54.390 --> 00:02:55.390
No.

00:02:55.390 --> 00:02:56.390
So there are holes there.

00:02:56.390 --> 00:03:04.000
Once you critically analyze it, Bernie wants
free healthcare and said, raise taxes to 52%

00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:06.849
on anyone making over $29,000.

00:03:06.849 --> 00:03:10.060
Am I looking at a primary source with Bernie's
tax tables?

00:03:10.060 --> 00:03:11.420
No, I'm not.

00:03:11.420 --> 00:03:13.610
Go to Bernie's website.

00:03:13.610 --> 00:03:15.499
Find his actual marginal tax tables.

00:03:15.499 --> 00:03:21.280
And you will see that if you are in $29,000,
you're not in the 52% marginal tax bracket.

00:03:21.280 --> 00:03:28.420
You need to earn $10 million per year to get
to Bernie's 52% marginal tax bracket.

00:03:28.420 --> 00:03:34.319
You can also simply apply critical thinking
and say, does it pass the sniff test that

00:03:34.319 --> 00:03:41.829
someone earning 29 grand a year would be paying
52% marginal tax rate?

00:03:41.829 --> 00:03:44.310
And the answer is of course no.

00:03:44.310 --> 00:03:51.010
So a very quick analysis of that meme, uh,
with the, with the sort of paradigm of caps

00:03:51.010 --> 00:03:56.939
critically analyze primary sources tells us
we are looking at non primary sources.

00:03:56.939 --> 00:04:00.209
It's an unsourced tweet from a right wing
celebrity.

00:04:00.209 --> 00:04:03.829
You are completely lacking critical analysis.

00:04:03.829 --> 00:04:08.859
Uh, if you simply accept it and that's the
only way that you fall for it.

00:04:08.859 --> 00:04:14.390
Now, if you want to look at more detailed
ways of for protecting yourself from disinformation

00:04:14.390 --> 00:04:18.829
propaganda, there are many strategies for
keeping emotions under control.

00:04:18.829 --> 00:04:24.370
When you see information, how to corroborate
it, evaluating whether conclusions that you

00:04:24.370 --> 00:04:29.740
are being told follow from the premises, actually
follow from the premises.

00:04:29.740 --> 00:04:30.740
But you can Google that.

00:04:30.740 --> 00:04:35.990
Google how to protect yourself from persuasion
propaganda, how to protect yourself from disinformation.

00:04:35.990 --> 00:04:41.120
There will be plenty of reading, but absent
all of that, always refer to when you see

00:04:41.120 --> 00:04:46.620
that tweet, when you see that Facebook post
from your right wing uncle, when you see Trump,

00:04:46.620 --> 00:04:51.580
say it at a rally caps, critically analyze
primary sources.

00:04:51.580 --> 00:04:57.349
Uh, once you have identified the primary sources,
and we will all be much, much better off

